Section: 4.3.2<br>
<br>
Original Text<br>
-------------<br>
=C2=A0 =C2=A0Servers which are authenticating with a PSK MUST NOT send the<=
br>
=C2=A0 =C2=A0CertificateRequest message in the main handshake, though they =
MAY<br>
=C2=A0 =C2=A0send it in post-handshake authentication (see Section 4.6.2) p=
rovided<br>
=C2=A0 =C2=A0that the client has sent the &quot;post_handshake_auth&quot; e=
xtension (see<br>
=C2=A0 =C2=A0Section 4.2.6).<br>
<br>
Corrected Text<br>
--------------<br>
=C2=A0 =C2=A0Servers which are authenticating with a resumption PSK MUST NO=
T send the<br>
=C2=A0 =C2=A0CertificateRequest message in the main handshake, though they =
MAY<br>
=C2=A0 =C2=A0send it in post-handshake authentication (see Section 4.6.2) p=
rovided<br>
=C2=A0 =C2=A0that the client has sent the &quot;post_handshake_auth&quot; e=
xtension (see<br>
=C2=A0 =C2=A0Section 4.2.6).=C2=A0 Servers which are authenticating with an=
 external PSK<br>
=C2=A0 =C2=A0MUST NOT send the CertificateRequest message either in the mai=
n handshake<br>
=C2=A0 =C2=A0or request post-handshake authentication. Future specification=
s MAY<br>
=C2=A0 =C2=A0provide an extension to permit this. <br>
<br>
Notes<br>
-----<br>
The lack of qualification on &quot;authenticating with a PSK&quot; implies =
that the statement applies equally to both external and resumption PSKs.=C2=
=A0 However, there are two conditions being governed: whether a certificate=
 can be requested during the handshake, and whether a certificate can be re=
quested post-handshake.=C2=A0 The latter of these requires different rules =
depending on the type of PSK.<br>
<br>
We know from the analysis of resumption (see <a href=3D"https://mailarchive=
.ietf.org/arch/msg/tls/TugB5ddJu3nYg7chcyeIyUqWSbA/" rel=3D"noreferrer" tar=
get=3D"_blank">https://mailarchive.ietf.org/arch/msg/tls/TugB5ddJu3nYg7chcy=
eIyUqWSbA/</a>) that combining a PSK handshake of either type with a client=
 certificate is not safe.=C2=A0 Thus, the prohibition on CertificateRequest=
 during the handshake applies equally to both resumption and external PSKs.=
<br>
<br>
For post-handshake, Appendix E.1 already discusses the risks of combining P=
SKs with certificates, citing the same analysis as above.<br>
<br>
=C2=A0 =C2=A0[...]=C2=A0 It is unsafe to use certificate-based client<br>
=C2=A0 =C2=A0authentication when the client might potentially share the sam=
e<br>
=C2=A0 =C2=A0PSK/key-id pair with two different endpoints.<br>
<br>
For this reason an external PSK is not safe to use with post-handshake auth=
entication.=C2=A0 A resumption PSK does not have this property, so the same=
 prohibition doesn&#39;t apply.<br>
<br>
Splitting the requirements as proposed makes this split clearer.<br>
<br>
